<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        THE CONTROLS THIS WINDOW IS MADE OF, EVERYWHERE EXCEPT THE LIST - the field somebody
        types into, the box somebody ticks, the chip and the items of the column picker. Not
        one value of its own: every number, colour and thickness below is a name declared in
        Values.xaml. The text itself - headings, sentences, steps, the count - left for
        Themes/Text.xaml on 2026-08-25.

        ADR-23, the same rule as the other two files.

        THIS FILE SHED THE LIST ON 2026-08-11 AND KEPT ITS NAME, because the name was always
        describing what is still here. Themes/List.xaml took the grid, the row template, the
        cell, the headings and the marks, unchanged and in the order they were in. It went
        because this file stood exactly on its 530-line ceiling and the configurable columns
        of S6d2 needed cell templates a resource dictionary could hand to a column built in
        code.

        THE media PREFIX WENT WITH THE LIST ON 2026-08-11 AND CAME BACK ON 2026-09-01, when the
        primary action's template needed a transparent fill for the state where it cannot be
        pressed. What the old sentence here was really protecting is the SYSTEM prefix, and that
        is still absent: the mutation entry proving a styles file holds no value has to inject a
        type the file can parse, so it injects a Thickness rather than a system:Double - which
        only works while neither styles file declares that prefix. Transparent is reached through
        x:Static against a NAME, the same idiom Chips.xaml and Overview.xaml use.

        NEITHER THIS FILE NOR List.xaml LOADS ON ITS OWN. Both reach WPF UI's keys and both
        reach ours in Values.xaml, and StaticResource resolves them WHILE THE FILE IS BEING
        READ. So the merge order in App.xaml is the library's dictionaries, then the value
        files, then Text.xaml, then this, then List.xaml.
    -->
    <!--
        EVERY TextBlock STYLE LEFT THIS FILE ON 2026-08-25, for Themes/Text.xaml, and the argument
        for the seam is at the head of that file. Backlog 213 named it six days earlier: this file
        stood at 395 lines against a markup ceiling of 397, so the next thing to go in needed
        something else to come out first.

        IT IS MERGED BEFORE THIS FILE RATHER THAN AFTER, and that is not a preference. The column
        picker's item template below reaches SubduedText through StaticResource, which resolves
        WHILE THIS FILE IS BEING READ - a dictionary merged after this one would be a name that
        does not exist yet.
    -->

    <!--
        The one field. Its padding is named rather than left to the framework because the hint
        sitting behind it has to line up with the text a person types, and the only way to be
        sure of that is for both to come from this line.

        BasedOn is the whole reason this comment is long. Without it a default style here does
        not ADD to the base one, it REPLACES it - so the field came out white, with grey hint
        text, on a dark window. It shipped that way from S6a until 2026-08-02, through a
        screenshot review that had the field on screen.

        Found by accident, while photographing this window to compare it against a control
        library. That is the fourth time in this project that XAML has bound correctly and
        painted something other than what it promised, after the empty list from internal view
        models, the column headers outside the visual tree, and the dead DataGridRow background.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultTextBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="CheckBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultCheckBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE COLUMN IN THE LIST OF COLUMNS SOMEBODY CHOOSES FROM - `A8`, arriving 2026-08-11.

        A MENU RATHER THAN A PANEL OF OUR OWN, AND THAT IS THE CHEAPEST HONEST SURFACE IN THIS
        WINDOW. A floating panel needs a fill, an edge and both of their contrast ratios argued in
        ContrastGuards, for a thing that is on screen while somebody clicks four times a year. A
        menu is already themed by the control library, is already used by this window for copying a
        name, and was already looked at by a person on the release build. Nothing new is declared
        here and no colour is invented.

        BasedOn OR THE LIBRARY IS INSTALLED AND INVISIBLE FOR THIS CONTROL - `docs/10` trap 4. An
        explicit style REPLACES an implicit one rather than adding to it, so without this line the
        items would come out in the built-in Windows look inside a Fluent window.

        BasedOn ON THE TYPE RATHER THAN ON A NAME, AND THAT IS MEASURED. Every other BasedOn in
        this product names one of their keys, so this one was written DefaultMenuItemStyle by
        analogy - and there is no such key. WindowGuards went red at once with the resource name in
        the message, which is the whole reason that guard builds a window rather than trusting a
        compile. Their assembly declares DefaultMenuStyle, DefaultToolTipStyle, DefaultButtonStyle
        and eighty others, and no named style for a menu ITEM: it is registered against the type.
        So the base is asked for the same way the framework asks for it.

        WHAT THIS COSTS AND WHY IT IS ACCEPTED: an implicit style is looked up by type at merge
        time, so this line goes quiet rather than red if the library ever stops registering one.
        A named key throws. Nothing here can be named, so the honest arrangement is to say so.

        StaysOpenOnClick, because turning on four columns is one errand rather than four. A menu
        that shuts after every tick makes a person open it four times to do one thing.

        Not one appearance value here, which is why this style is allowed to say nothing about how
        it looks: what a column is CALLED, whether it is on, and whether it may be turned off are
        all answered by the choice it is bound to.
    -->
    <!--
        THE ONE THING ON A SCREEN THAT MOST WANTS PRESSING - 2026-09-01 evening, owner's decision
        after the palette study, and the colour's own argument is beside SurfacePrimaryAction in
        Colours.xaml. What belongs here is why this is a TEMPLATE rather than three setters.

        BECAUSE THREE SETTERS WERE TRIED AND THE OWNER SAW THEM FAIL. The button off the first
        screen wore the accent as a local value on Background - and went DARK under the pointer,
        because the library's template answers hover by painting the border it template-binds, and
        a value on the Button loses to a setter inside the template it is drawn by. That is the same
        family as `docs/10` trap 16 and it is why every family this product invents now owns its
        template outright.

        THE COST IS THE SAME AS THE CHIP'S AND SO IS THE ANSWER: hover, pressed, disabled and focus
        are this file's job, all four are below, and ButtonGuards holds them.

        DISABLED MATTERS MORE HERE THAN ANYWHERE ELSE IN THIS WINDOW. The button at the foot of a plan is disabled
        exactly when the session cannot carry anything out, which is the moment somebody most needs
        to be told - so it gives up the fill entirely rather than dimming it. An accent that is
        merely a bit darker reads as a button that is still waiting to be pressed.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="PrimaryActionFocusRing">
        <Setter Property="Control.Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate>
                    <Border BorderBrush="{StaticResource FocusRing}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource FocusRingThickness}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}" />
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="PrimaryAction" TargetType="Button">
        <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{StaticResource PrimaryActionFocusRing}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />

        <!--
            THE SIZE IS NAMED BECAUSE THE WEIGHT IS, and TypeScaleGuards went red the moment it was
            not. Microsoft's guidance puts the floor for SEMIBOLD text at 14 against 12 for plain,
            so a style that asks for the heavier stroke and leaves the size to whatever contains it
            is asking for a floor it cannot answer for. Fourteen is also what the buttons beside
            this one are set at, which is the other half of why it is right.
        -->
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadButton}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <Border x:Name="Face"
                            Background="{StaticResource SurfacePrimaryAction}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource SurfacePrimaryAction}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </Border>

                    <!--
                        ORDER IS THE BEHAVIOUR: disabled comes last so it beats a pointer resting on
                        a button that cannot be pressed.
                    -->
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfacePrimaryActionHover}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="BorderBrush"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfacePrimaryActionHover}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfacePrimaryActionPressed}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="BorderBrush"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfacePrimaryActionPressed}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="BorderBrush"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE ONE BUTTON THAT CHANGES A MACHINE, 2026-09-02, ON THE OWNER'S WORDS THAT IT IS ONE OF THE
        TWO THAT MATTER. It is the primary action with a red fill rather than a template of its own,
        which is the whole claim: it must behave identically and read differently, and a second
        template would be a second place for hover, pressed and disabled to drift apart.

        WHY RED IS SPENT HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. Red on this window now means three things and each
        wears a different form: this FILL says "pressing this changes your machine", red TEXT says
        "you cannot act", and a red OUTLINE says "here is the way out of that sentence". Give the
        elevate button a red fill too and the three collapse into one - a status line where the
        sentence saying nothing can be done and the button that fixes it are the same colour, side
        by side. The numbers are beside SurfaceDestructiveAction in Colours.xaml.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="DestructiveAction" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource PrimaryAction}">
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <Border x:Name="Face"
                            Background="{StaticResource SurfaceDestructiveAction}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource SurfaceDestructiveAction}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </Border>

                    <!-- Order is the behaviour, the same as the blue one: disabled comes last. -->
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceDestructiveActionHover}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="BorderBrush"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceDestructiveActionHover}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceDestructiveActionPressed}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="BorderBrush"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceDestructiveActionPressed}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="BorderBrush"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE WAY OUT OF THE RED SENTENCE, 2026-09-02. An outline in the colour of the words it answers
        rather than a fill, so it reads as belonging to them - and it is the only button in this
        window with a coloured outline, which is what keeps it the loudest thing in that row without
        borrowing the one meaning the fill above owns.

        IT WAS THE BLUE PRIMARY ACTION UNTIL TODAY, which made the status line say "this is the main
        thing to do here" in a window whose main thing is the list.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ElevateAction" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource PrimaryAction}">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                    <Border x:Name="Face"
                            Background="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}"
                            BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}"
                            CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHover}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        EVERY TOOLTIP IN THIS WINDOW WRAPS - backlog 167, and it is the third answer to a question
        the owner asked twice.

        WHAT WAS WRONG. CellText binds a cell's tooltip to its own text, which is how a truncated
        cell can still be read in full. Three columns carry lists - dependencies, triggers,
        required privileges - and the longest privilege list on this machine holds 28 names, about
        700 characters. A tooltip does not wrap on its own, so it arrived as ONE LINE ACROSS HALF
        THE SCREEN: the control that exists to make a long value readable was the least readable
        thing on the screen.

        BasedOn IS NOT OPTIONAL AND THAT IS TRAP 4 OF `docs/10`. A style with a TargetType the
        control library also styles REPLACES theirs outright rather than adding to it - so a
        tooltip declared without this line would lose their background, border and shadow and come
        out as black text on nothing. DefaultToolTipStyle is the name they give it.

        IMPLICIT RATHER THAN KEYED, so it reaches the ones nobody would remember to opt in: the
        cells, the marks in the start column, the search box's own tip. A keyed style would have
        wrapped the cells and left the eleven others exactly as they were.

        THE TEMPLATE IS WHAT ACTUALLY WRAPS. MaxWidth alone does not - a tooltip whose content is
        a string measures that string on one line and then gets clipped, which looks like the
        width being ignored. The content has to be presented by something that knows how to break
        a line, so it is a TextBlock with TextWrapping.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="ToolTip" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultToolTipStyle}">
        <Setter Property="MaxWidth" Value="{StaticResource WidthToolTip}" />

        <Setter Property="ContentTemplate">
            <Setter.Value>
                <DataTemplate>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                </DataTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
